# Where a DO/LOOP header's clause boundaries fall, which is the axis a
# flattened header can get wrong and no shape case asks about.
#
# A loop header has more boundaries than a branch does, and they are not the
# same event:
#
#   * the DO clause's own, which the header's expressions run inside;
#   * one per pass for the re-test, whose clause is whichever instruction
#     transferred control back to the loop -- the DO on the first pass, the END
#     on a fall-through, an ITERATE that cut a pass short;
#   * UNTIL's own, which is a separate decision point from the top-of-loop one.
#
# Each row queues a CALL ON handler from one of those places and observes where
# it is delivered, because a promoted clause that opens a boundary and does not
# discharge it is invisible to every test that only asks what a loop computes.
#
# The requeue rows are about a boundary leaving MORE work behind it. A delivered
# handler can itself RAISE ... RETURN, queueing a second trap, and a clause
# boundary drains only the entries that were queued when it began -- so that
# second trap is not one the boundary that delivered the first owes, and that
# boundary is not the last one with work. The SIGL of the second delivery says
# which boundary took it WHENEVER THE CANDIDATE BOUNDARIES SIT ON DIFFERENT
# LINES, which is what a row here has to arrange; where two of them share a line,
# SIGL names the line and not the boundary.
#
# Other rows are about the header's own ordering rather than its boundaries,
# and they are here because the compiled form is what makes the ordering a
# thing that can break: each header value is evaluated, echoed and validated
# before the next value is evaluated at all.
#
# EVERY EXPECTED BYTE BELOW WAS MEASURED AGAINST THE C++ ORACLE. That the two
# engines produce identical bytes for a row is asserted rather than written
# down here: `both_engines_agree_on_every_case_file` (`tests/ir_recorded.rs`) runs
# every stanza of this directory on both and compares them, so a row that
# stopped agreeing reddens instead of contradicting a sentence nobody rereads.
# A row that differs from the oracle says so in its
# own comment. Regenerate a row by running its program under the oracle, never
# with REWRITE=1 -- ir_recorded.rs refuses to run at all under REWRITE for that
# reason. Capture is:
#
#   ( ulimit -v 1048576; LD_LIBRARY_PATH=<ooRexx>/build/lib \
#     <ooRexx>/build/bin/rexx PROGRAM.rex </dev/null )
#
# LOOP-HEADER BOUNDARIES THAT DIVERGE FROM THE ORACLE ARE NOT ROWS HERE,
# because a row's expected block is what this crate prints and these are places
# where that is not what the oracle prints. Each was measured on both engines,
# and the engines agree on each; none moved when the header was flattened.
#
#   * **An `UNTIL` requeue.** The oracle delivers the second trap at the `END`'s
#     own clause, `G ran 5`; this crate delivers it at the clause after the loop,
#     `G ran 6`. **This bullet names no program, so its observation cannot be
#     re-run and its cause is not established -- read the withdrawal below
#     before relying on any of it.** (What stood here attributed it to the
#     `END` being an instruction the oracle executes and this crate never
#     steps.)
#
# **The `UNTIL` bullet's cause is NOT established, and the bullet cannot be
# checked, because it does not name its program.** It was filed under the
# elided-instruction family -- the oracle runs a clause boundary at a position
# where this crate has no instruction to run one at. **That attribution is
# withdrawn rather than replaced, and the reason is narrow: nothing reproduces
# this bullet's own observation.** Reconstructing a program from its words has
# been tried repeatedly and produced a different answer every time, none of
# them the bullet's, so there is no run to attribute a cause to.
#
# **The elided-instruction mechanism itself is real, and is not what is being
# withdrawn.** Task 6's Step 1c names a program that demonstrates it on a
# different construct: an empty `do` / `end` with a double requeue, where the
# oracle delivers the second condition at the `END`'s own boundary (`K ran 6`)
# and this crate has no boundary there to deliver it at. What is unsupported is
# this bullet, not the mechanism it was filed under.
#
# The bullet stays, because it records a real observation someone made; its
# numbers are left alone, because choosing which program produced them would be
# adding one more reconstruction and filing it as the bullet's own.
#
# **The `DO UNTIL` row below is not this bullet's program and is not offered as
# one.** That row prints `H ran 6`, `after set` and `G ran 7`, which is what the
# oracle prints for it; this bullet records `G ran 5` for the oracle against
# `G ran 6` for this crate. So a `DO UNTIL` row agreeing below neither confirms
# this bullet nor closes it.
#
# They are recorded here rather than in a report because this is
# the file the next reader of a loop-header boundary opens.
#
# Expected lines are tagged so that no line of the block can be empty: an empty
# line ends an expected block, and a program's own output may hold one.
#
# `/nonexistent/ir-dual-case.rex` is `ir_recorded.rs`'s own INLINE_PATH, which a
# raised condition's middle line prints. It is written out rather than stood in
# for, because these blocks are compared verbatim and nothing substitutes into
# them; running the same program under the oracle prints the oracle's own path
# there instead.

# a call on handler queued by a TO expression
# The handler is queued while the header is being evaluated and has to run
# before the first body clause reads the variable it sets -- both passes print
# `set`. A header whose clause never ends would print `unset` twice.
program
call on user zx name h
zv = 'unset'
do i = 1 to raiser()
  say 'body' zv
end
say 'after' zv
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 2
h:
zv = 'set'
return
----
rc> 0
out> body set
out> body set
out> after set

# a handler queued by a TO expression that queues again
# The premise a single boundary per construct gets wrong: `h` runs at the
# header's boundary and its own RAISE queues `zy`, which nothing at that same
# boundary delivers. SIGL 4 is the first body clause, so the second delivery is
# the *next* boundary rather than the header's own -- and a header that
# delivered both would report 3.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
do i = 1 to raiser()
  nop
end
say 'after'
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 1
h:
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> G ran 4
out> after

# the same requeue with zero passes
# The row above with `raiser` returning 0 instead of 1, so the loop runs no
# pass and no body clause stands between the two deliveries. SIGL 6 names the
# `say` after the loop, not the `end` on line 5 and not the `do` on line 3, and
# `after` prints before `G ran 6` rather than after it. The row above, with one
# pass, reports `G ran 4` and prints it first, so it does not cover this shape.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
do i = 1 to raiser()
  nop
end
say 'after'
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 0
h:
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> after
out> G ran 6

# a call on handler failing at a DO header's own boundary
# The failure the handler raises is attributed to the clause whose boundary ran
# it, which is the DO's own -- the defect one construct over was a promoted IF
# recording its own failure site but not its boundary's.
#
# **The handler's own line is the oracle's; the enclosing frame's is not.**
# The oracle prints `     2 *-*   do i = 1 to raiser()` for the frame the
# handler was delivered from, two columns further in, because its traceback
# reads each activation's live indent counter and the `DO` block is still open
# there. This crate reports that frame at the `DO` clause's own recorded
# failure site instead, which carries no block level. That is a traceback
# quantity rather than a transfer one -- the line above it, the handler's own
# clause, agrees byte for byte.
#
# **A `SELECT CASE` diverges the same way, in the same single line.** Measured
# on both engines, `trace r` / `call on user zx name h` / `select case
# raiser()` with a handler that divides by zero -- so `select case` is line 3
# -- the enclosing frame's traceback line:
#
#   oracle       3 *-*   select case raiser()
#   this crate   3 *-* select case raiser()
#
# The handler's own two lines above it agree byte for byte, so this is the same
# family and the same reason: the `SELECT`'s block is still open where the
# oracle's traceback reads the counter, and this crate's recorded failure site
# carries no block level.
#
# **A `SELECT CASE`'s clause boundary is the same rule one construct over.**
# `SELECT` is a block instruction too, so the oracle's counter is already one
# deeper when that clause ends, and `Interp::settle_block_indent` is what both
# constructs reach. A trap queued by a `WHEN` condition settles at the level
# its clause echoed at instead, because a `WHEN` is not a block instruction.
# Both are held against the oracle's own bytes in `tests/trace_indent.rs`.
#
# **A plain `SELECT`'s own boundary is still two columns short, and it is this
# same defect on the construct that has no `CASE`.** A boundary delivers
# whatever is pending rather than only what its own clause queued: a `CALL ON`
# handler ending in `raise ... return` leaves a second condition for the next
# boundary to take, and a plain `select` standing there is what takes it. The
# program, whole, because a transcript without one cannot be regenerated:
#
#   trace r                       | say 'after' zn
#   call on user zx name h        | exit
#   call on user zy name g        | raiser1:
#   zn = raiser1()                | raise user zx return 5
#   select                        | h:
#     when 1 = 1 then say 'w'     | raise user zy return 1
#     otherwise say 'o'           | g:
#   end                           | say 'G ran' sigl
#                                 | return
#
# read down the left column then the right, so `select` is line 5 and `g:` is
# line 15. Both engines print `G ran 5` -- `SIGL` naming the `select` clause's
# own line, which is what says it is that boundary delivering -- and stdout and
# `rc 0` agree throughout. What does not is the handler's activation, every
# line of it two columns short:
#
#   oracle                            this crate
#       15 *-*     g:                     15 *-*   g:
#       16 *-*     say 'G ran' sigl       16 *-*   say 'G ran' sigl
#          >>>       "G ran 5"               >>>     "G ran 5"
#       17 *-*     return                 17 *-*   return
#
# `select case 1` is the same program with a scrutinee that queues nothing
# either, and it agrees, because `Interp::select_case` settles the boundary and
# a caseless `SELECT` never reaches it. **So what decides is whether anything
# settled the boundary, never what the clause queued** -- which is why "its
# clause queues nothing" is not a reason to leave a block instruction's
# boundary unsettled.
#
# **A DIFFERENT FAMILY, AND IT IS CLOSED.** The same requeue route pointed at a
# plain `do` / `end` is not an indent and not a trace quantity: it is the
# printed order and the printed `SIGL`, on **stdout**, with no `TRACE` anywhere
# in the program.
#
# **A plain `DO` has a clause at its header and a clause at its `END`, and the
# oracle ends each where this crate now does.**
# `RexxInstructionSimpleDo::execute` traces the instruction, opens the block
# and returns without running the body, so the header's clause ends before any
# body instruction does; `END` is an instruction of its own, so it reaches a
# boundary of its own. A condition pending when the `DO` runs is therefore owed
# to the `DO`'s line, and one requeued inside the block is owed to the `END`'s.
#
# **The step that spans the whole construct is this crate's own scaffolding and
# owes no boundary at all.** A `DO`/`LOOP` resolves every pass inside one
# `step`, and a boundary at the end of that step is a boundary the oracle does
# not have. It shows on a `DO UNTIL`, whose `END` boundary the `UNTIL` test has
# already served: the requeue is owed to the clause *after* the loop, and a
# boundary belonging to the step delivers it early instead. That shape runs the
# opposite way to every other one here, which is why it is named rather than
# left to follow from the others.
#
# `corpus/lang/do_clause_boundaries.rex` holds the family against the live
# oracle -- the header, the `END`, the empty-body control that separates them,
# the `DO UNTIL`, and the loops and neighbouring constructs the rule must not
# move. The stanzas below pin the tree-walker's own bytes for the same shapes,
# which the corpus runner does not: it runs this crate at its default engine.
#
# **`if ... then` and `SELECT` are on this route and are NOT this defect.**
# `Select`'s own arm wraps just the `CASE` evaluation in `in_clause` and closes
# it before the `WHEN`s run, so that boundary is correctly placed. The plain
# `SELECT` entry above stands as written: measured, its handler activation is
# still two columns short of the oracle's on both engines, and the rule above
# does not reach it.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
zv = 'unset'
zw = raiser()
do
say 'body'
end
say 'after' zv zw
exit
raiser:
raise user zx return 5
h:
zv = 'set'
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> G ran 5
out> body
out> after set 5

# The `END`'s own boundary, which only a second requeue reaches. The first
# delivery is owed to the `do` on line 5 and the second to the `end` on line 6,
# so both land before the `say` that follows the block.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
call on user zw name k
zn = raiser1()
do
end
say 'after' zn
exit
raiser1:
raise user zx return 5
h:
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
raise user zw return 1
k:
say 'K ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> G ran 5
out> K ran 6
out> after 5

# The `DO UNTIL`, which runs the other way. The `UNTIL` test is the `END`'s own
# clause and takes the first delivery at line 6; the requeue is owed to the
# `say` on line 7, so that line prints before its handler rather than after.
program
call on user zx name h
call on user zy name g
zv = 'unset'
do until raiser1() > 0
nop
end
say 'after' zv
exit
raiser1:
raise user zx return 5
h:
say 'H ran' sigl
zv = 'set'
raise user zy return 1
g:
say 'G ran' sigl
return
----
rc> 0
out> H ran 6
out> after set
out> G ran 7

